1. PyUltraLight unit system (PyUltraLight / PyUltraLight 2 / PyUL_SK)

   Eig_ULDM_packages/units.py (convert, convert_back, convert_between with PyUL's unit strings, the physical
   constants and the code-unit definitions) is adapted from the corresponding functions of PyUltraLight /
   PyUltraLight 2 as found in PyUltraLight2/Current_SK.py of PyUL_SK, and Eig_ULDM_packages/pyul_run.py
   copies the same constants and code-unit definitions verbatim, so that the toolkit reproduces PyUL's
   units exactly. Eig_ULDM_packages/pyul_run.py also reads the run files
   (config.uldm, reproducibility.uldm, Outputs/...) in the formats written by PyUltraLight 2 and PyUL_SK.

   These conventions come from
   - PyUltraLight: F. Edwards, E. Kendall, S. Hotchkiss and R. Easther, "PyUltraLight: A Pseudo-Spectral
     Solver for Ultralight Dark Matter Dynamics", JCAP 10 (2018) 027, doi:10.1088/1475-7516/2018/10/027,
     arXiv:1807.04037;
   - PyUltraLight 2 / PyUL_NBody: Y. Wang and collaborators (Auckland Cosmology),
     https://github.com/Sifyrena/PyUL_NBody, doi:10.5281/zenodo.6336160;
   - PyUL_SK (the fork used for the simulations of the Stone Skipping paper): A. Zhang,
     https://github.com/Ailun-Zhang/PyUL_SK.

2. Soliton profile (Eig_ULDM_packages/soliton.h5)

   The dataset /profile of soliton.h5 is identical, bit for bit, to PyUltraLight's standard soliton profile
   "Soliton Profile Files/initial_f.npy" (900000 samples, dr = 1e-5), as distributed with PyUltraLight,
   PyUltraLight 2 and PyUL_SK. The file stores it with the attributes dr, beta and mass0 (mass0 rounded to
   3.883; see docs/conventions_and_errata.md, E3).

3. Eigenmode method

   The eigenfunction solver methodology and the reference soliton workflow were inspired by Mathematica code
   provided by J. Luna Zagorac. No Mathematica code is distributed here. The theory of the eigenmode
   decomposition is J. L. Zagorac, I. Sands, N. Padmanabhan and R. Easther, "Schroedinger-Poisson solitons:
   perturbation theory", Phys. Rev. D 105, 103506 (2022), doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.105.103506.

4. Dependencies

   The toolkit uses, but does not include, NumPy, SciPy, Matplotlib, h5py, tqdm and pyshtools
   (https://shtools.github.io/SHTOOLS/); each is distributed under its own licence.

